          'Quick-Service Restaurants (QSRs) and fast food franchises operate under intense operational pressures that few other foodservice venues ever encounter. Between 11:30 AM and 1:30 PM, a busy dual-lane drive-thru and self-service dining room can process between 180 and 350 transactions per hour. With over 75% of those customer orders including a large 32 oz or 44 oz fountain beverage, ice is not merely a refreshing amenity—it is the structural backbone of high-margin beverage sales.',
          'In a standard fast-food fountain cup, ice fills between 40% and 55% of the total liquid volume. A 32 oz fountain drink requires approximately 0.7 to 0.8 lbs of ice. During a two-hour lunch rush, a high-volume fast food franchise can easily exhaust 300 to 450 lbs of ice from its dispenser hopper alone. If your ice machine cannot keep pace, drive-thru transaction times spike, fountain beverages are served lukewarm causing carbon dioxide breakout and flat soda, and customer satisfaction plunges.',
          'To sustain this frantic pace, modern fast-food restaurants require commercial ice systems capable of producing between 1,200 and 2,500 lbs of ice every 24 hours. Furthermore, these machines must operate continuously in hot kitchen environments (often exceeding 95°F ambient room temperature near fryers and broilers) without tripping high-pressure safety switches or suffering thermal lockouts.',
