          'Because commercial kitchens are rich in airborne yeasts, starches, and warm ambient moisture, fungal spores settle into cold drain pipes. Over time, bacteria and fungal yeasts form a thick, gelatinous pink or brown biofilm colloquially known as "ice machine slime." This slime accumulates inside horizontal drain line runs, trapping mineral sediment until the pipe is completely choked.',
